From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000006 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gino (WLTB-Gino) <gino@wltechblog.com>
Subject: [PATCH] motor: drain debounce timers before GPIO teardown in remove()

motor_remove() tore things down in an order that leaves windows for
use-after-free / NULL-deref on module unload and reload:

- del_timer_sync() ran after gpio_free() and after motor->pdata = 0,
  so an already-armed min/max debounce timer (armed from the GPIO
  hardirq, firing 5ms later from softirq; the callbacks dereference
  motor->pdata and gpio_get_value() the min/max pin) could dereference
  released data and freed pins. del_timer_sync() now runs first, per
  motor, before any GPIO in that motor is freed or pdata is cleared.
- gpio_free() ran before the matching free_irq(), so the IRQ could
  fire one last mod_timer() against a GPIO that is already freed.
  Order inside each motor is now del_timer_sync() -> free_irq() ->
  gpio_free() -> clear state.
- ingenic_tcu_counter_stop() ran while /dev/motor was still registered, so a
  racing open/ioctl could drive a TCU channel that is being stopped. It now
  runs after misc_deregister(): the counter stops only once no opener can
  reach the device.
- mutex_destroy() is dropped entirely (option A): dev_mutex is a debug-only
  construct here (CONFIG_DEBUG_MUTEXES adds runtime checks, production
  builds compile it to a no-op; the mutex is never re-initialized anywhere),
  so the init/remove cycle stays balanced. The residual unregister-races-
  teardown window (userspace can still open /dev/motor before
  misc_deregister() returns) is the generic chardev teardown race present
  throughout this SDK - closing it fully would need the probe/remove rework
  the vendor driver never had, not a reorder.

Companion to 0005 (devres double-free); together they close the
teardown races seen around runtime motor.ko reload on noorio t110.
Regenerated against the restructured thingino/ingenic-sdk layout
(4.4.94/misc/motor/motor.c).
Signed-off-by: Gino (WLTB-Gino) <gino@wltechblog.com>
--- a/4.4.94/misc/motor/motor.c
+++ b/4.4.94/misc/motor/motor.c
@@ -1014,21 +1014,23 @@ static int motor_remove(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	int i;
 	struct motor_device *mdev = platform_get_drvdata(pdev);
 	struct motor_driver *motor = NULL;
-	ingenic_tcu_counter_stop(mdev->tcu);
-	mutex_destroy(&mdev->dev_mutex);
-
 	free_irq(mdev->run_step_irq, mdev);
 	for(i = 0; i < HAS_MOTOR_CNT; i++) {
 		motor = &(mdev->motors[i]);
 		if(motor->pdata == NULL)
 			continue;
+		/* Drain the debounce timers before touching the GPIOs: the
+		 * callbacks dereference motor->pdata and gpio_get_value() the
+		 * min/max pins, so both must stay valid until no timer can run. */
+		del_timer_sync(&motor->min_timer);
+		del_timer_sync(&motor->max_timer);
 		if (motor->min_pos_irq) {
-			gpio_free(motor->pdata->motor_min_gpio);
 			free_irq(motor->min_pos_irq, motor);
+			gpio_free(motor->pdata->motor_min_gpio);
 		}
 		if (motor->max_pos_irq) {
-			gpio_free(motor->pdata->motor_max_gpio);
 			free_irq(motor->max_pos_irq, motor);
+			gpio_free(motor->pdata->motor_max_gpio);
 		}
 		if (motor->pdata->motor_st1_gpio != -1)
 			gpio_free(motor->pdata->motor_st1_gpio);
@@ -1046,14 +1048,13 @@
 		motor->max_pos_irq = 0;
 		motor->min_pos_irq_cnt = 0;
 		motor->max_pos_irq_cnt = 0;
-		del_timer_sync(&motor->min_timer);
-		del_timer_sync(&motor->max_timer);
 	}
 
 	if (mdev->proc)
 		proc_remove(mdev->proc);
 
 	misc_deregister(&mdev->misc_dev);
 
+	ingenic_tcu_counter_stop(mdev->tcu);
 	return 0;
 }
